## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world constraints: on-device adaptation, occlusion robustness, and AR-friendly latency for indoor property inspections. Probes data strategy and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose from RGB
- On-device 5-shot adaptation
- Synthetic + real data strategy
- Occlusion-robust tracking
- Edge GPU latency targets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: simple adaptation step
function adapt(batch) {
  for (const [x, y] of batch) {
    const pred = model.predict(x);
    const loss = poseLoss(pred, y);
    optimizer.step(loss);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you compare on-device adaptation strategies under varying illumination?
- What metrics best detect drift in furniture appearance over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a new angle: multi-sensor fusion with event cameras for high-dynamic scenes. Assesses architectural discipline and concrete trade-offs for low latency and on-device adaptation.

## Key Concepts
- Event cameras and RGB fusion
- 3D voxel grids and lightweight transformers
- On-device 5-shot adaptation and object priors
- Temporal consistency and occlusion handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo code: fuse RGB features with events, then predict pose and mask
function forward(rgbFrame, events, priors){
  const rgbFeat = rgbBackbone(rgbFrame);
  const evtFeat = eventBackbone(events);
  const fused = fuse3D(rgbFeat, evtFeat);
  const pose = poseHead(fused, priors);
  const mask = segHead(fused);
  return {pose, mask};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you design synthetic data generation to cover unseen shapes with realistic event streams?
- What debugging metrics would help you diagnose latency vs. accuracy bottlenecks on edge hardware?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests real-time monocular depth, multi-task learning, and pose estimation in a challenging forest domain, plus on-device few-shot adaptation and map fusion.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular depth and semantic/instance segmentation
- 6-DoF pose estimation for markers
- TSDF fusion for map building on edge hardware
- Differentiable rendering for domain adaptation
- On-device 5-shot adaptation with temporal consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-architecture sketch
class MonocularDroneNet {
  constructor(){ /* backbone */ }
  forward(frame){
    const depth = this.depthHead(frameFeatures)
    const segPose = this.segPoseHead(frameFeatures)
    const pose = this.markerPoseHead(segPose)
    const map = this.tsdfFuse(depth, pose)
    return {depth, segPose, pose, map}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ablate temporal consistency vs per-frame independence?
- What data policies ensure robust marker generalization across weekly styles?
- How would you monitor edge latency under CPU/GPU contention?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine learned features with classical cues under tight on-device latency, in a realistic retail robotics setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight CNN + classical feature fusion
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-5 shots)
- Latency budgeting (<=50 ms/frame)
- Robust evaluation: per-item accuracy, misorientation metrics, throughput

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of forward pass
function forward(frame) {
  const rois = detectROIs(frame);
  for (const roi of rois) {
    const cnnFeat = smallCNN(roi);
    const cues = extractCues(roi); // hist, edges, texture
    const fused = fuse(cnnFeat, cues);
    const label = orientationHead(fused);
    if (label === 'misoriented') alert(roi);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label drift with new item designs?
- What ablation studies would you run to verify cue contributions?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a real-time monocular system integrating segmentation, 3D pose estimation with scene priors, grasp-point planning, and physics-based stability reasoning—plus efficient edge adaptation with minimal shots.

## Key Concepts

- Monocular 3D pose estimation using environmental priors
- Grasp-point prediction in cluttered scenes
- Differentiable physics modules for stability evaluation
- On-device few-shot adaptation strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for the model heads
function forward(img, shelfPrior) {
  const features = backbone(img);
  const segmentation = segmentationHead(features);
  const pose = poseHead(features, shelfPrior);
  const grasp = graspHead(features, pose);
  const stability = physicsPrior(grasp, pose);
  return { segmentation, pose, grasp, stability };
}
```
